# Frontend environment. Copy to `.env.local` (which is gitignored) and edit.
#
#   Copy-Item frontend/.env.example frontend/.env.local
#
# Everything here is OPTIONAL — the app works with none of it set.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Demo logins on a production build
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The login screen shows tap-to-fill demo account chips. They are normally visible ONLY in
# dev builds, so a real deployment never advertises credentials.
#
# Why you probably want this ON for a demo: offline page reload and PWA install only work
# against the production build (`npm run demo`, port 4173) — the dev service worker
# precaches nothing. So the demo runs from a production build, where the chips are hidden by
# default, and nobody wants to type a 10-digit phone number on stage.
#
# NEVER set this on anything publicly reachable.
VITE_SHOW_DEMO_LOGINS=true

# Password shown in the demo hint text. Cosmetic only — it does not change any account.
# VITE_DEMO_PASSWORD=demo1234

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API location
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEAVE THIS UNSET unless you know you need it.
#
# Unset, requests are same-origin `/api/...` and Vite proxies them to localhost:4000 — for
# both the dev server (5173) and the preview server (4173). That is what makes LAN access
# work for the two-laptop demo with no reconfiguration: the proxy runs on the same machine
# as the API, so a phone hitting http://192.168.x.x:4173 still reaches it.
#
# Setting it bypasses the proxy and goes cross-origin, which then requires that exact origin
# to be listed in the backend's CORS_ORIGINS. Only set it when the API is genuinely on a
# different host (e.g. a deployed backend).
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://swasthbharat-api.onrender.com
#
# REQUIRED when deploying to Firebase Hosting. Hosting serves only static files — there is
# no backend behind it and no dev-server proxy in production, so without this every API
# call and the Socket.io connection would target Hosting's own origin and 404. Set it to
# wherever the Express API actually runs, and add that Hosting origin to the backend's
# CORS_ORIGINS. See README.md "Deploying".

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Firebase phone sign-in (OPTIONAL)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave all of this unset and the login screen is exactly as it always was: mobile number
# and password. The SMS-code section only appears when the config below is complete AND
# VITE_ENABLE_PHONE_SIGNIN is 'true'.
#
# Why it is behind a second flag rather than just the config: the same Firebase project is
# also used for push notifications, and a project can have Cloud Messaging working while
# phone auth is disabled or out of SMS quota. Without the flag, that combination shows a
# button that always fails, which is worse than no button.
#
# The Firebase SDK is dynamically imported, so it is a separate chunk and is NOT added to
# the offline precache. A worker who never uses SMS sign-in never downloads it.
#
# These five values are NOT secrets. They identify the project and ship in every Firebase
# web app; what protects the project is the authorised-domain list plus the backend's token
# verification. The service-account private key is a secret and lives only in backend/.env.
# From the Firebase console: Project settings -> General -> Your apps -> SDK setup.
# VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
# VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=your-project.firebaseapp.com
# VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your-project
# VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=
# VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=

# Turns the SMS-code section on. Requires the five values above.
# VITE_ENABLE_PHONE_SIGNIN=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Background push notifications (OPTIONAL, independent of phone sign-in)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Adds a "Phone alerts" toggle to the doctor dashboard. Socket.io already delivers a
# high-risk case to an OPEN dashboard instantly; this covers the case where the tab is
# closed. It does not replace the live path.
#
# Needs the five VITE_FIREBASE_* values above PLUS this key, and PUSH_NOTIFICATIONS_ENABLED
# on the backend. The two Firebase features are independent, so each has its own switch.
#
# Firebase console -> Project settings -> Cloud Messaging -> Web Push certificates ->
# "Key pair". This is the PUBLIC half of a VAPID pair; the private half stays with Firebase.
# VITE_FIREBASE_VAPID_KEY=

# Notes worth knowing before testing this:
#  - Requires a secure context. localhost counts; a plain-HTTP LAN address does NOT, so
#    testing from a phone at http://192.168.x.x will report the feature as unsupported.
#  - Two service workers are in play. The Workbox one (offline reload) stays at scope '/';
#    Firebase registers public/firebase-messaging-sw.js at its own narrow scope. They do not
#    interact, which is deliberate — the offline worker is verified working and is not
#    touched by this feature.
#  - The 245 kB Firebase SDK is a separate chunk and is excluded from the offline precache
#    (see manualChunks + workbox.globIgnores in vite.config.ts). A notification feature
#    cannot work offline, so caching it for offline use would be pure cost.

# ---- Local development against the Auth emulator ----
# No Firebase project, no SMS quota, no network:
#   Terminal 1:  firebase emulators:start --only auth
#   Terminal 2:  npm run dev:web
# The emulator prints the OTP to its own console instead of sending an SMS.
# Set the matching FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST in backend/.env too, or the backend will
# reject the emulator's unsigned tokens.
# VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:9099

# ---- Demoing on a real project without burning SMS quota ----
# Firebase console -> Authentication -> Sign-in method -> Phone -> "Phone numbers for
# testing". Add each seeded demo number with a fixed code (e.g. +919800000001 / 123456).
# Those numbers then never send a real SMS and always accept the fixed code, which is what
# makes the seeded accounts usable on stage.
